The problem keys solve, verified:

```js
// WITHOUT keys (positional matching):
// [A, B, C] → [Z, A, B, C]   (Z inserted at front)
// React matches by POSITION: position-0 was A, now Z.
// Every node is torn down and rebuilt → all state lost, all inputs reset.

// WITH key={item.id} (identity matching):
// React sees A, B, C still exist (same keys) → their nodes are PRESERVED,
// only Z is created and inserted at the front. 3 nodes reused (verified).

{todos.map(todo => (
  <TodoRow key={todo.id} todo={todo} />   // stable identity from data
))}
```

The rules for good keys:
- Unique among siblings (not globally — two lists can both have key='a')
- Stable across renders (the same item keeps the same key)
- From the data (database id, slug) — NOT the array index

Why index keys are the trap (the interview's real question): index is stable only while the list never reorders. Insert at front → every item's index shifts → keys stop matching → React rebuilds everything. Worse: with stateful rows (an input inside each row), index matching makes row-3's state appear in what is now row-3 but is different data — user types in one row, deletes the row above, and their text 'moves' to a different item. Index keys are safe only when the list is static, never reordered, and rows have no state.
